WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:07.890
- Okay.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:09.393
Welcome everybody.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.060
I'm Maurice Samuels,

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.160
the director of the Yale Program

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:16.110
for the Study of Antisemitism,

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.050
and it's my great pleasure
to welcome you today

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:24.050
to this hybrid lecture,
both in person and on Zoom.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.960
It's our first attempt to
do a hybrid format here.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:32.640
So the way it's gonna work is

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.360
that I will introduce Professor Feinberg.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.420
He's gonna speak for about 40 minutes,

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.880
and then we'll take 20
minutes of questions

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.480
from the audience, both
in person and on Zoom.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.370
So, those of you on Zoom,

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:51.540
we ask you to submit your
questions using the Q&amp;A function

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:56.220
at the bottom of your screen,
the chat function is disabled.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.270
And then we will ask
in-person, Professor Feinberg,

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:04.530
your questions that you
submit in the Q&amp;A function.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.240
And you can submit those questions

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.790
at any time during the lecture.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:11.040
You don't have to wait until the end.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:14.760
So, I'll now introduce Professor Feinberg.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.760
Ayal Feinberg is one of
the leading researchers,

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.300
doing quantitative
analysis of antisemitism,

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.990
an extremely valuable
contribution to the field.

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:28.230
He is associate professor
of political science

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.510
and antisemitism studies,

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.480
and is the director of the
Center for Holocaust Studies

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.940
and Human Rights at Gratz College,

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.130
overseeing the largest holocaust

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.923
and genocide graduate
program in the world.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:44.100
His research has been widely featured

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:47.550
in leading international
journals and media outlets,

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.980
and his scholarship is frequently utilized

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:53.190
by practitioners and
policymakers concerned

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:56.040
with the insecurity of marginalized groups

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:59.370
and the perseverance of
the Jewish community.

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:01.137
In addition to all of this,

00:02:01.137 --> 00:02:05.160
and in addition to his academic position,

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.830
he's also a quantitative research fellow

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:09.960
at the Anti-Defamation League Center

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.940
for Antisemitism Research.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.760
And he recently completed
a three-year term

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:16.890
as the junior faculty chair

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.650
at the Academic Engagement Network.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:23.550
His antisemitic bias incident
analysis was inducted

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:25.320
into the congressional record,

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.740
during the 2019 House
Judiciary Committee's hearing

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.505
on hate crime and the
rise of white nationalism.

00:02:32.505 --> 00:02:34.957
His talk today is entitled,

00:02:34.957 --> 00:02:38.190
"Clarifying Antisemitism
Through Experiments:

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:41.550
Exploring Blame,
Responsibility, and Hate Crime."

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:44.943
So please join me in welcoming
back to Yale, Ayal Feinberg.

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:53.603
- Thank you for

00:02:53.603 --> 00:02:57.450
that very, very kind
introduction, Professor Samuels.

00:02:57.450 --> 00:02:59.400
Very excited to be back at Yale

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:02.370
to be presenting some of
the experimental research

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:05.790
that I'm doing along with my
colleague Dr. Jacob Scott Lewis

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:08.070
at Washington State University.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:11.010
A lot of this research
is pretty new for us,

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:12.390
and we're really excited to begin

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:14.400
to share this research with the public.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:18.330
So we really welcome all of
your questions, your comments,

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:19.890
and ways that we can improve upon

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.660
and build on this research.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:24.420
So the title, as Dr. Samuel said,

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:27.270
is "Clarifying Antisemitism
Through Experiments:

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:31.677
Exploring Blame,
Responsibility, and Hate Crime."

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:45.750
Sorry, everyone, just
getting the clicker ready.

00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:48.270
Okay, I think it's critical

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:51.960
to talk about why I'm
engaged in this process.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.180
Although I'm proud that my research is

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.670
as objective as I'm able to design it,

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:00.030
I am motivated by an
overarching normative concern

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:02.243
about violent antisemitism.

00:04:02.243 --> 00:04:04.641
And I think that these
three images share a lot

00:04:04.641 --> 00:04:07.920
of important elements that
we need to be thinking

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.190
about when we're constructing
anti-Semitism research today.

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:13.200
I think most people are
familiar with this image

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:17.460
on the right of the Tree
of Life Synagogue Memorial,

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:18.960
outside of the Tree of Life,

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:22.080
after the most lethal antisemitic attack

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:25.084
on American Jews in our country's history.

00:04:25.084 --> 00:04:29.760
On the left is the AMIA
attack in Buenos Aires,

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.350
an attack that most American Jews,

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:35.760
are far less familiar with,
but resulted in 87 fatalities.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:37.441
Many considered this attack

00:04:37.441 --> 00:04:42.270
to be the most lethal antisemitic
attack post-Holocaust.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:45.360
And what's really critical
here as we struggle

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.240
with understanding the role
of anti-Israel sentiment

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:51.030
and anti-Zionism and
explaining antisemitism,

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:53.190
is a image from the riots that happened

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:55.740
in South France in 2014,

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:59.580
where Jewish businesses
and Jewish individuals,

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.200
are indiscriminately targeted

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:03.333
because of the behavior of Israel.

00:05:11.963 --> 00:05:14.675
Clicker struggles continue.

00:05:14.675 --> 00:05:16.475
Sorry, everyone, give me one second.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.890
Okay, success, all right.

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:27.890
There is concern surrounding
rising antisemitism here

00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:29.760
in the United States.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.930
There is a general feeling
that Jews are less safe.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:35.190
How do we know this?

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:36.390
What are the empirics

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:40.620
that we're utilizing to
base this information on?

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:43.230
Well, the AJC has done a survey

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:44.940
of the American Jewish population

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:48.180
where they ask several questions

00:05:48.180 --> 00:05:53.070
that help us to determine the
security of Jewish Americans.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:56.613
One of the questions that they
frequently ask in the survey,

00:05:57.847 --> 00:05:59.990
"Do you think that
antisemitism is a problem

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:01.680
in the United States?"

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:03.780
From the most recent
iteration of the survey,

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:06.900
89% of Americans say that
antisemitism is a problem

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:08.340
in the United States.

00:06:08.340 --> 00:06:11.430
In fact, this number hovers between

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:14.288
about 80 and 95% in any given year.

00:06:14.288 --> 00:06:17.010
It doesn't tell us a great deal

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:20.250
at an individual level
about Jewish insecurity,

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:21.870
but what I think helps us

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:25.713
to articulate the current
situation quite greatly is

00:06:25.713 --> 00:06:28.440
that 82% of American Jews believe

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:30.120
that antisemitism has increased.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:34.290
There is a feeling that there
is increasing antisemitism.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:37.020
These numbers are higher
than many previous years,

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:41.940
and that 41% of American Jews
report feeling less secure,

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:43.413
than a year ago.

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.380
Only 4% feel more secure.

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:50.310
Again, these numbers seem to show

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:53.820
that there is a new increased feeling

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:56.610
of insecurity amongst American Jews.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:59.640
The other thing that we often do is look

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:01.680
to the surveys where we ask

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:05.370
about antisemitic trope alignment.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:10.370
So the ADL has offered up
many surveys since 1964,

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:13.050
using the same question battery aimed

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:16.950
at measuring the number of
antisemitic trope alignment

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:19.650
that exists amongst the
American population.

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:20.670
What do we see?

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:25.293
A big difference, from 2019 to 2022.

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:31.050
This gap, 20% of Americans constitute

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:34.323
about 54 million Americans that now fall

00:07:34.323 --> 00:07:36.870
into the category of those that align

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:40.050
with at least six of the
11 antisemitic tropes

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:41.253
that the ADL measures.

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:45.960
Okay, so a little bit about
what I'm going to be doing today

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:47.250
in this presentation,

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:49.650
I wanna do a little bit
of housekeeping first.

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:51.830
I never know exactly who my audience is,

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:55.050
so I think it's important
to do a brief overview

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:57.360
of antisemitism, and share a little bit

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:59.820
about antisemitism empirics.

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:02.280
I'm gonna talk to you and
provide you an overview

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:05.160
of some of the experimental literature

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:07.890
that already examines antisemitism.

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:11.580
And then I'm gonna share
two of my own experiments

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:12.480
that I've done, again,

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:15.450
with Jacob Scott Lewis
at Washington State,

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:18.900
exploring blame, attribution
and responsibility to speak out

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:21.990
as well as the effects
of hate crime exposure

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:23.970
on foreign policy preferences.

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:25.350
And then I'm gonna tease everybody

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:27.100
with a little bit of future survey.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:31.380
So let's start with defining antisemitism.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:34.500
A tremendous amount of time, resources,

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:38.910
and concern surround how
do we define antisemitism.

00:08:38.910 --> 00:08:40.803
It's one of these enduring questions.

00:08:41.670 --> 00:08:46.473
On the left is the IHRA
definition of antisemitism.

00:08:47.310 --> 00:08:50.670
Seen by many as a gold standard,

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:54.180
or a way that institutions
can adopt a definition

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:57.000
that best protects Jews in part,

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.630
because of how IHRA
provides specific examples

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:04.713
of when anti-Zionism
constitutes antisemitism.

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:09.600
On the right is the
ADL's definition, right?

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:12.090
The ADL, one of the leading
organizations concerned

00:09:12.090 --> 00:09:13.790
with Jewish safety and insecurity.

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:16.830
Happy to go over some of the differences

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:18.687
that we see in these
definitions in the Q&amp;A,

00:09:18.687 --> 00:09:20.310
but I wanted to make sure

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:23.553
that we were provided a
definition of antisemitism.

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:29.460
Every presentation that
I give on antisemitism,

00:09:29.460 --> 00:09:32.550
I include this quote
from historian populists,

00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:34.380
because I think it's critical

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:37.830
that when we evaluate
antisemitism, we understand

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:42.420
that no perpetrator
ideology owns antisemitism.

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:44.970
As we see antisemitism being weaponized

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:46.860
on the left and the right,

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:50.370
it's important to remember
that antisemitism manifests

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:53.100
in a multitude of ways.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:55.530
Jews are always showing off.

00:09:55.530 --> 00:09:57.600
They are hermetic and secretive.

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:59.430
They will not assimilate.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:01.470
They assimilate only too well.

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:03.120
They are too religious,

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:06.600
they're too materialistic,
and a threat to religion.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:09.660
They're uncultured, they
have too much culture.

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:13.290
They avoid manual work,
they work too hard.

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:17.190
They are miserly, they're
ostentatious spenders.

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:19.890
They're inveterate capitalists,

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:23.610
they're born communist, and so on.

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:26.160
In all its myriad manifestations,

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:27.870
the language of antisemitism

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:30.570
through the ages is
dictionary of non-sequiturs

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:34.953
and antonyms, a thesaurus of
illogic and inconsistency.

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:39.630
What are these enduring
questions that I mentioned?

00:10:39.630 --> 00:10:41.280
Things that we struggle with

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:44.010
that we don't yet have
generalizable answers to,

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:47.790
but are absolutely
critical in how we seek,

00:10:47.790 --> 00:10:50.403
measuring, and tracking antisemitism.

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:55.460
First and foremost, who is a Jew?

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:58.470
Is being Jewish something that's scalable

00:10:58.470 --> 00:10:59.643
or is it binary?

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:03.540
Understanding this is absolutely critical,

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:04.830
and there is, again,

00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:08.940
no generalizable agreement
on what constitutes this.

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:11.430
Most survey research, a lot of
the work that I'm gonna share

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:12.370
with you today

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:17.520
that explores specifically
Jewish perceptions,

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:18.870
much of this comes from the idea

00:11:18.870 --> 00:11:21.183
of self-reporting one's Jewish identity.

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:26.280
What is antisemitism?

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:28.350
How do we define antisemitism?

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:31.800
Should we even use the term antisemitism?

00:11:31.800 --> 00:11:35.017
AJC did some recent
polling on this that said,

00:11:35.017 --> 00:11:38.583
"An enormous proportion of Americans,

00:11:38.583 --> 00:11:41.970
near the majority of Americans,

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:44.217
don't know how to define antisemitism."

00:11:45.277 --> 00:11:46.727
I haven't really heard of it.

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:50.850
So perhaps, we should push
towards using alternative terms

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:53.700
that most Americans
would be able to identify

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:56.190
as representing Jew hate.

00:11:56.190 --> 00:11:57.683
There's a movement to
suggest that we should,

00:11:57.683 --> 00:12:01.683
instead of using antisemitism,
use the term, Jew hate.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.541
Of course, I mentioned
the IHRA definition,

00:12:05.541 --> 00:12:08.250
we saw a lot of concern regarding this,

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:11.970
when Biden rolled out his new
plan to combat antisemitism.

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:14.073
What definition should we use?

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:18.650
Because if we're not all
utilizing the same definition,

00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:21.333
we're not all calling the
same balls and strengths.

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:24.270
Should we be using JDA?

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:27.690
We have Nexus, we have
several other definitions

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:30.060
of antisemitism that we should consider.

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:34.530
And then of course, anti-Israel,
anti-Zionist beliefs,

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:37.980
when does holding specific
anti-Israel sentiment

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:41.820
or when does manifestation of
specific anti-Zionist beliefs,

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:45.123
actually constitute antisemitism if ever?

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:47.913
No generalizable agreement here.

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:49.740
Other things that I think

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:51.940
we should consider are
measurement concerns.

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:54.390
We do a lot of research.

00:12:54.390 --> 00:12:56.610
Most of the tracking that we see,

00:12:56.610 --> 00:13:01.610
most of what Americans are
exposed to are either allies

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:06.180
of hate crimes or some
of the survey results

00:13:06.180 --> 00:13:07.683
that I already shared today.

00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:10.980
Attitudes and behavior.

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:14.730
While we like to think that
these are connected and linked,

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:19.050
in reality, their relationship
is far less clear.

00:13:19.050 --> 00:13:20.700
So we need to be exploring research

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:23.280
that really connects specific attitudes

00:13:23.280 --> 00:13:26.253
to particular manifestations
that we're most concerned.

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:30.660
What about antisemitism and philosemitism,

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:34.173
philosemitism being those that love Jews?

00:13:35.820 --> 00:13:38.850
These two phenomenon are not only linked,

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:40.830
sometimes when you measure them,

00:13:40.830 --> 00:13:43.350
they show up in the exact same ways.

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:44.970
Some people have gone as hard to say

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.510
that philosemitism in and of itself,

00:13:47.510 --> 00:13:50.130
is a form of antisemitism.

00:13:50.130 --> 00:13:52.480
So it's critical as
we're trying to decipher

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:55.860
what constitutes antisemitism,

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:58.677
are we measuring antisemitism,

00:13:58.677 --> 00:14:02.400
are we measuring philosemitism,
are these terms the same?

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:04.230
And especially, in the United States,

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:06.120
what many have called the
most philosemitic country

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:07.950
in the world, this is an important thing

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:11.550
that we have yet to truly
figure out in the discipline.

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:14.397
And then of course, latent antisemitism.

00:14:15.243 --> 00:14:17.610
When we think about racism,

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:21.690
even those that are the most
virulent racists and bigots,

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:24.210
they don't always wear
that on their sleeve.

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:27.243
It doesn't define their
every action and opportunity.

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:33.600
Often, it takes a certain stimulus

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:38.160
to induce an element
of racism and bigotry.

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:40.470
Much of antisemitism is like,

00:14:40.470 --> 00:14:42.900
so how do we measure a phenomenon

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:46.413
that isn't necessarily
empirically available for us?

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:50.730
I also wanna talk a little bit

00:14:50.730 --> 00:14:53.310
about general measures of antisemitism.

00:14:53.310 --> 00:14:56.910
When it comes to our choices
in doing quantitative research,

00:14:56.910 --> 00:14:58.650
we can look at events.

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:01.140
This often constitutes acts of terror,

00:15:01.140 --> 00:15:04.530
hate crime variation,
bias incident variation.

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:09.003
We can look at social media
methods on tweets, YouTube,

00:15:10.530 --> 00:15:15.090
Facebook, TikTok, not
just the content itself,

00:15:15.090 --> 00:15:17.250
not just content frequency,

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:20.160
but the responsiveness of
these various platforms

00:15:20.160 --> 00:15:23.520
in removing the content,
how quickly they remove it.

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:26.280
Are they removing it
for certain actors only?

00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:30.030
Does it take a specific
act to report the content

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:32.139
in order to remove it?

00:15:32.139 --> 00:15:33.870
And this has become very
important, of course,

00:15:33.870 --> 00:15:36.690
with Elon Musk's takeover of Twitter, X,

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:45.033
and his concern regarding
the ADL in that fact.

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:47.340
Policy.

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:49.860
We can look at governmental
discrimination.

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:51.633
Here in the United
States, we'd like to think

00:15:51.633 --> 00:15:54.330
that there is no longer
governmental discrimination,

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:59.070
targeting Jews, but across
Europe and in many other places,

00:15:59.070 --> 00:16:02.583
Jews are not always able to
practice their religion freely.

00:16:03.510 --> 00:16:07.260
Societal discrimination and
institutional discrimination,

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:10.160
are other outlets that we can
use to measure antisemitism.

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:13.650
There's interesting work
that's now being done

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:15.150
through network analysis,

00:16:15.150 --> 00:16:18.030
looking at organizational connections,

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:21.960
looking at group mapping,
looking at extremist networks.

00:16:21.960 --> 00:16:24.603
How are hate groups organizing?

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:28.440
How are they building content?

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:30.570
How are they building followers?

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:33.384
And then of course, attitudes and beliefs.

00:16:33.384 --> 00:16:35.550
What we commonly evaluate through surveys,

00:16:35.550 --> 00:16:38.733
but also through interviews,
focus groups, and experiments.

00:16:43.710 --> 00:16:46.230
We are having a few technical issues.

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:50.580
Okay, common descriptive
approaches, right?

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:53.907
What we most frequently see, surveys.

00:16:53.907 --> 00:16:56.280
One of the biggest
challenges we have here is

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:58.440
that antisemitism, as I mentioned,

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:01.410
is an extremely challenging
phenomena to measure.

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:04.920
In 2022, the ADL changed its battery

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:07.680
that hadn't changed since 1964.

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:09.605
We added three questions

00:17:09.605 --> 00:17:12.690
and we changed some of the balances.

00:17:12.690 --> 00:17:13.593
What do we see?

00:17:14.490 --> 00:17:15.900
At the very top,

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:19.140
when asking a nationally
representative sample,

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:23.017
70% of Americans say that,

00:17:23.017 --> 00:17:26.370
"Jews stick together more
than other Americans."

00:17:26.370 --> 00:17:28.980
Report that's at least
mostly or somewhat true.

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:33.980
At the bottom, 16% mostly say
mostly are somewhat say that,

00:17:35.587 --> 00:17:38.427
"Jews are not as honest
as other business people."

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:43.233
Antisemitism is multi-factor.

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:46.530
There are many ways in
which it can manifest.

00:17:46.530 --> 00:17:47.940
So any survey that attempts

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:50.880
to measure it has these challenges.

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:53.943
How do we measure all of these
elements of antisemitism?

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:57.000
We can look at incident reporting,

00:17:57.000 --> 00:18:00.180
and the ADL audit report is
something that's very common.

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:01.710
The FBI's hate crime report

00:18:01.710 --> 00:18:03.540
that comes out annually is another way

00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:06.960
that we can evaluate
antisemitic incident variation

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:09.270
in antisemitism in the United States.

00:18:09.270 --> 00:18:12.480
The tremendous amount of money is going

00:18:12.480 --> 00:18:16.440
to fund interventions
and to generate policy

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:18.510
that combats antisemitism

00:18:18.510 --> 00:18:22.080
through these primarily
descriptive approaches.

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:24.390
What I contend is that
we need to go further.

00:18:24.390 --> 00:18:27.180
We need to do more rigorous
quantitative analysis.

00:18:27.180 --> 00:18:29.719
We need to understand the mechanisms

00:18:29.719 --> 00:18:33.510
that explain why certain
attitudes are more likely

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:35.730
to lead to antisemitic behavior.

00:18:35.730 --> 00:18:37.020
Through the experimental work

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:39.210
that I and others are pursuing,

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:41.520
I'm hoping that that's gonna be the case.

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:43.260
So why experiments?

00:18:43.260 --> 00:18:46.140
Experiments offer us a really unique way

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:48.510
to isolate specific factors

00:18:48.510 --> 00:18:51.813
and correlates we think explain
variation in antisemitism.

00:18:53.430 --> 00:18:54.810
It helps us to evaluate

00:18:54.810 --> 00:18:57.453
what are the structures of antisemitism.

00:18:58.620 --> 00:19:01.650
In some cases, as I mentioned
with latent antisemitism,

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:04.563
what are the conditions
that elevate antisemitism?

00:19:05.850 --> 00:19:08.520
What's the relationship between attitudes

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:10.080
and policy preferences?

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:12.150
Something I'm gonna share
today with our research

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:14.790
that explores hate crime exposure,

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:18.990
and how does an exposure to a
specific narrative or event,

00:19:18.990 --> 00:19:23.583
all to belief in a traditional
marketing sense message test?

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:27.240
It's important to note, right,

00:19:27.240 --> 00:19:29.910
as I promote an experimental approach

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:31.410
to understanding antisemitism,

00:19:31.410 --> 00:19:34.800
there are shortcomings that
we are constantly consider;

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:35.820
the pollster effect

00:19:35.820 --> 00:19:38.490
or what many have known
as interviewer effect.

00:19:38.490 --> 00:19:41.700
The emergence of specific
characteristics of the messenger

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:45.480
or the deliverer of a certain
question altering the way

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:47.973
in which a respondent approaches it.

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:52.050
One of the ways that you
can do that is to make sure

00:19:52.050 --> 00:19:55.680
that you have multiple
different characteristics

00:19:55.680 --> 00:19:57.420
of any sort of enumerator,

00:19:57.420 --> 00:19:59.427
any person that's responsible
for delivering it,

00:19:59.427 --> 00:20:00.660
and to evaluate whether

00:20:00.660 --> 00:20:04.410
or not there is this
messenger effect in place.

00:20:04.410 --> 00:20:05.463
A bogus pipeline.

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:09.480
A way to avoid some of the
social desirability concerns

00:20:09.480 --> 00:20:11.013
of a potential respondent.

00:20:12.420 --> 00:20:14.790
Let them know that in fact

00:20:14.790 --> 00:20:18.090
we are able to detect when they're alive.

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:20.400
And then of course, pure
control and vignette control.

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:22.710
This is something my
colleague, Jacob Scott Lewis,

00:20:22.710 --> 00:20:25.438
and I are very interested in is whether

00:20:25.438 --> 00:20:28.980
or not we should be looking
simply at a pure control,

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:32.130
those that aren't exposed
to any sort of treatment,

00:20:32.130 --> 00:20:35.250
or whether we should also
provide an innocuous treatment,

00:20:35.250 --> 00:20:38.130
and whether or or seemingly
innocuous treatment

00:20:38.130 --> 00:20:41.163
and whether or not that actually
creates different findings.

00:20:42.630 --> 00:20:44.850
So I'm gonna go over briefly now,

00:20:44.850 --> 00:20:49.620
some of the most important
pieces of experimental literature

00:20:49.620 --> 00:20:51.753
in evaluating antisemitism.

00:20:53.130 --> 00:20:57.010
This is largely focused on
the work of Michal Bilewicz

00:20:58.350 --> 00:21:00.060
at University of Warsaw,

00:21:00.060 --> 00:21:02.580
who is the leading expert when it comes

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:04.533
to antisemitism experimentation.

00:21:05.400 --> 00:21:10.290
So one of the main beliefs
is that it's important

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:14.520
for us as scholars to create
typologies of antisemitism.

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:18.547
What are the factors that
explain antisemitism?

00:21:18.547 --> 00:21:21.330
Bilewicz et al. in 2013,

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:24.810
focus on authoritarian
personality characteristics.

00:21:24.810 --> 00:21:27.810
They focus on victimhood-based
social identity.

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:30.240
They focus on relative deprivation.

00:21:30.240 --> 00:21:32.950
And they examine whether
or not this manifests

00:21:35.639 --> 00:21:38.820
in these factor loadings here of focusing

00:21:38.820 --> 00:21:41.460
on a three-factor
structure of antisemitism,

00:21:41.460 --> 00:21:44.700
conspiratorial antisemitism,
traditional religious

00:21:44.700 --> 00:21:49.050
and anti-Judaic beliefs, and
secondary antisemitic beliefs,

00:21:49.050 --> 00:21:52.080
those that focus on
Holocaust commemoration.

00:21:52.080 --> 00:21:56.490
They looked at a population
in Poland to do this work.

00:21:56.490 --> 00:21:58.800
What they're able to find is

00:21:58.800 --> 00:22:02.760
that specific factors of antisemitism help

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:07.090
to explain variation in
how somebody would choose

00:22:08.370 --> 00:22:11.099
to donate resources

00:22:11.099 --> 00:22:15.480
to commemorating certain
elements of history.

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:18.930
Specifically that antisemitic beliefs

00:22:18.930 --> 00:22:22.350
and holding of antisemitic
beliefs, the possession of it,

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:24.030
and the strength of those beliefs,

00:22:24.030 --> 00:22:29.030
help to make one choose
to provide those resources

00:22:30.390 --> 00:22:33.750
to a museum that's not
associated with the Holocaust,

00:22:33.750 --> 00:22:35.820
but instead of broader national museum.

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:36.933
So what this shows here,

00:22:36.933 --> 00:22:40.560
what the summary shows is that those

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:44.400
that hold traditional antisemitic beliefs,

00:22:44.400 --> 00:22:46.650
secondary antisemitic beliefs,

00:22:46.650 --> 00:22:48.840
and belief in Jewish conspiracy,

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:52.923
all donate less money
to Jewish organizations.

00:22:55.470 --> 00:22:59.280
A lot of concern is focused
on the contact hypothesis.

00:22:59.280 --> 00:23:00.330
For those that are not familiar

00:23:00.330 --> 00:23:02.370
with the contact hypothesis originating

00:23:02.370 --> 00:23:03.693
with all court in 1954,

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:09.710
what Stefaniak et al. do
in 2022 is a meta-analysis

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:14.640
of eight different studies
evaluating active engagement

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:18.300
with historical ethnic
diversity in Poland.

00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:22.260
The idea here was to provide
a specific curriculum

00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:25.923
for students across Polish high schools,

00:23:26.880 --> 00:23:30.030
and to evaluate whether or not engaging

00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:35.030
in this curriculum with Jews
helps to reduce antisemitism.

00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:41.280
Participation in the
intervention contributes

00:23:41.280 --> 00:23:44.820
to building knowledge and
interest in local history,

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:47.850
more positive attitudes
towards Jewish people,

00:23:47.850 --> 00:23:50.580
and toward ethnic diversity in general.

00:23:50.580 --> 00:23:53.130
Attendees of this program,

00:23:53.130 --> 00:23:56.490
developed stronger place attachment

00:23:56.490 --> 00:23:59.070
in civic engagement intentions.

00:23:59.070 --> 00:24:00.870
But their general social trust

00:24:00.870 --> 00:24:05.870
and attitudes toward other
ethnic groups, the Roma people,

00:24:05.940 --> 00:24:08.160
remained largely unchanged.

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:11.610
So this element, this
program, this curriculum,

00:24:11.610 --> 00:24:14.670
works in the reduction of antisemitism,

00:24:14.670 --> 00:24:16.980
but it is not nearly as effective

00:24:16.980 --> 00:24:19.533
in reducing broader forms prejudice.

00:24:21.330 --> 00:24:23.490
A lot of individuals are concerned

00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:26.370
with the notion of power and antisemitism,

00:24:26.370 --> 00:24:29.973
but also powerlessness and antisemitism.

00:24:31.230 --> 00:24:33.663
What happens when people
lose their agency?

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:38.493
What happens when there's a lack of power,

00:24:39.420 --> 00:24:42.990
a lack of control and
feelings of uncertainty?

00:24:42.990 --> 00:24:44.130
So first and foremost,

00:24:44.130 --> 00:24:47.147
we know that lack of
control and uncertainty

00:24:47.147 --> 00:24:50.523
in the political domain
are highly interconnected.

00:24:52.560 --> 00:24:54.030
Political uncontrollability,

00:24:54.030 --> 00:24:56.430
rather than political uncertainty,

00:24:56.430 --> 00:25:00.600
predicts individual's beliefs
in Jewish conspiracies.

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:04.443
So, what happened in this case
with this research design?

00:25:05.400 --> 00:25:07.710
One group was given a vignette

00:25:07.710 --> 00:25:11.340
that focuses on political
uncontrollability.

00:25:11.340 --> 00:25:13.680
The other was given a vignette

00:25:13.680 --> 00:25:16.500
that looks at political uncertainty.

00:25:16.500 --> 00:25:18.030
And what they find is

00:25:18.030 --> 00:25:21.630
that it's the uncontrollability
not uncertainty,

00:25:21.630 --> 00:25:24.600
it's the lack of agency that helps

00:25:24.600 --> 00:25:28.203
to explain individual's
beliefs in Jewish conspiracies.

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:32.670
Political uncontrollability, they find,

00:25:32.670 --> 00:25:34.950
predicts belief in Jewish conspiracy,

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:36.780
even after controlling

00:25:36.780 --> 00:25:39.810
for antisemitic discriminatory attitudes,

00:25:39.810 --> 00:25:42.363
even when you control proprietor beliefs.

00:25:44.670 --> 00:25:46.770
The same study also shows

00:25:46.770 --> 00:25:50.250
that lack of control may
be specific particularly

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:52.380
to conspiracy stereotypes of Jews,

00:25:52.380 --> 00:25:54.810
and less specific to groups not viewed

00:25:54.810 --> 00:25:58.350
as prototypical conspiring agents.

00:25:58.350 --> 00:26:02.733
It's specific to Jews,
not Germans, not Russians.

00:26:03.930 --> 00:26:06.450
There's a direct
relationship between feelings

00:26:06.450 --> 00:26:09.930
of lack of control and
general conspiracies,

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:11.948
but that belief is weak,

00:26:11.948 --> 00:26:12.781
and there's an increase

00:26:12.781 --> 00:26:15.150
in the endorsement of
conspiracy stereotypes

00:26:15.150 --> 00:26:19.473
of Jews driving other conspiracy beliefs.

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:24.813
Great work from Antoniou
et al. in 2015 and 2020,

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:28.440
focusing on the Greek context.

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:30.900
They're really interested
in understanding this notion

00:26:30.900 --> 00:26:33.873
of competing and collective victimhood.

00:26:33.873 --> 00:26:37.110
Antisemitism stems from a
sense of collective victimhood,

00:26:37.110 --> 00:26:40.500
defined as a perception
that an individual's nation,

00:26:40.500 --> 00:26:42.330
has been a victim of history

00:26:42.330 --> 00:26:44.760
that is rooted in the
historical narrative found

00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:48.960
in education, and other socialization age.

00:26:48.960 --> 00:26:50.790
So, what do they do?

00:26:50.790 --> 00:26:53.203
Right, they provide specific vignettes

00:26:53.203 --> 00:26:56.010
that are aimed at increasing a sense

00:26:56.010 --> 00:26:58.653
of comparative victimhood or collectively.

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:03.270
What they find is that
inducing ingroup favoritism,

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:04.680
leads any group members

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:07.053
to downplay the collective
suffering of others.

00:27:08.460 --> 00:27:11.970
This manifests through
antagonism with other groups,

00:27:11.970 --> 00:27:16.500
widely acclaimed as victims
over the moral benefits emerging

00:27:16.500 --> 00:27:17.763
from victimhood status.

00:27:19.020 --> 00:27:24.020
So to put it simply, there's
a competition over victimhood

00:27:24.180 --> 00:27:26.480
that they're identifying
through this process.

00:27:29.970 --> 00:27:33.540
Now, I think this is an
experiment that really is useful

00:27:33.540 --> 00:27:35.490
for understanding a lot
of what's manifesting

00:27:35.490 --> 00:27:38.670
in America today as it
relates to hate speech,

00:27:38.670 --> 00:27:41.463
as it relates to antisemitic
social media content.

00:27:42.660 --> 00:27:45.990
The Soral et al. piece
shows that lower sensitivity

00:27:45.990 --> 00:27:50.310
to hate speech, was a positive mediator

00:27:50.310 --> 00:27:52.800
of the relationship
between frequent exposure

00:27:52.800 --> 00:27:55.173
to hate speech and outgroup prejudice.

00:27:56.070 --> 00:27:59.370
They provided desensitization
trait, and what they found is

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:03.210
that after you desensitize
somebody to hate speech,

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:06.330
individuals were less
sensitive to hate speech

00:28:06.330 --> 00:28:10.410
and more prejudice towards
hate speech victims,

00:28:10.410 --> 00:28:12.930
than their counterparts that
were in the control condition.

00:28:12.930 --> 00:28:15.693
Those that did not receive
that desensitization drink.

00:28:16.800 --> 00:28:19.410
The effects of exposure to
hate speech on prejudice,

00:28:19.410 --> 00:28:23.061
were mediated by a lower
sensitivity to hate speech,

00:28:23.061 --> 00:28:25.803
and not buy lower
sensitivity to social norms.

00:28:27.030 --> 00:28:30.180
And the last piece that I
wanna show you before going

00:28:30.180 --> 00:28:33.810
into my own work is the
mortality salience piece

00:28:33.810 --> 00:28:35.010
and the double standards piece

00:28:35.010 --> 00:28:38.303
that was done by Florette
Cohen et al. in 2009.

00:28:39.300 --> 00:28:41.310
The effect of mortality salience,

00:28:41.310 --> 00:28:43.110
and when we say mortality salience,

00:28:43.110 --> 00:28:46.770
the idea of bringing up the idea of death.

00:28:46.770 --> 00:28:49.860
The effect of mortality
salience on increasing support

00:28:49.860 --> 00:28:52.680
for punishing countries that
commit human rights violations,

00:28:52.680 --> 00:28:56.070
was most strongly apparent
in the Israel condition.

00:28:56.070 --> 00:28:59.313
So, when you expose somebody
to this idea of death,

00:29:00.270 --> 00:29:04.200
it manifests with specific
anti-Israel sentiments

00:29:04.200 --> 00:29:08.760
and an increase in antisemitism.

00:29:08.760 --> 00:29:12.990
Because antisemitism may
increase hostility to Israel,

00:29:12.990 --> 00:29:15.090
it's hard to parse things out.

00:29:15.090 --> 00:29:17.220
And this is some of what
I'm trying to do now

00:29:17.220 --> 00:29:19.260
with my experimental work.

00:29:19.260 --> 00:29:21.450
So my work is long focused

00:29:21.450 --> 00:29:24.270
on what we call the
homeland-diaspora nexus,

00:29:24.270 --> 00:29:27.660
understanding the role
of Jewish attachment

00:29:27.660 --> 00:29:29.700
and identity as it relates to Israel,

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:31.630
but also how Israeli behavior

00:29:32.700 --> 00:29:37.110
and perceptions of Israeli
behavior affect the identity

00:29:37.110 --> 00:29:39.093
and insecurity of Jews.

00:29:39.093 --> 00:29:40.590
This is really a combination

00:29:40.590 --> 00:29:42.690
of what I'd say are several disciplines,

00:29:42.690 --> 00:29:45.270
international relations, antisemitism,

00:29:45.270 --> 00:29:47.160
and those that focus on hate crimes,

00:29:47.160 --> 00:29:50.703
law and criminology,
and a lot in sociology.

00:29:51.990 --> 00:29:53.280
The main research question

00:29:53.280 --> 00:29:54.990
that I'm interested in answering is,

00:29:54.990 --> 00:29:58.740
what is Israel's role
in explaining variation

00:29:58.740 --> 00:30:01.293
in antisemitic events and hate crimes?

00:30:04.440 --> 00:30:07.350
I did a piece in 2019

00:30:07.350 --> 00:30:10.260
that really explored this in
the European context, right?

00:30:10.260 --> 00:30:11.940
We've seen a lot as it relates

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:14.460
to some descriptive statistics

00:30:14.460 --> 00:30:18.480
about spikes in antisemitism
when Israel's engaged

00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:20.583
in a violent conflict in the Middle East.

00:30:21.540 --> 00:30:24.960
Operation protective edge,
looking at France and UK here,

00:30:24.960 --> 00:30:28.080
we see a massive spike in the month

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:30.480
that Israel's engaged in that operation,

00:30:30.480 --> 00:30:34.440
only to decline back to
normal levels just a month

00:30:34.440 --> 00:30:35.273
or two later.

00:30:36.300 --> 00:30:40.920
When I looked at data from 2009 to 2017,

00:30:40.920 --> 00:30:45.180
evaluating any month
where Israeli casualties

00:30:45.180 --> 00:30:47.883
from war exceeded 100,

00:30:48.810 --> 00:30:53.703
we see a 35% rise in
antisemitic hate crimes

00:30:53.703 --> 00:30:57.693
that are reported across
28 European states.

00:30:59.490 --> 00:31:01.893
I also looked at this
in the American context,

00:31:04.110 --> 00:31:06.260
the most philosemitic
country in the world.

00:31:07.110 --> 00:31:08.700
What did we find?

00:31:08.700 --> 00:31:10.980
We found that Israeli military operations,

00:31:10.980 --> 00:31:14.760
the weeks in which Israeli
military operations took place,

00:31:14.760 --> 00:31:17.370
was the most important substantive factor

00:31:17.370 --> 00:31:20.040
in explaining antisemitic
incident variation here

00:31:20.040 --> 00:31:21.333
in the United States.

00:31:23.310 --> 00:31:27.660
Total incidents increased by
roughly 24% in those weeks,

00:31:27.660 --> 00:31:28.920
but what's critical here is

00:31:28.920 --> 00:31:33.920
that violent incidents
increased by 33%, right?

00:31:34.260 --> 00:31:38.241
There's a connection
between Israeli violence

00:31:38.241 --> 00:31:41.853
and antisemitic violence
here in the United States.

00:31:43.440 --> 00:31:44.790
And then on a college campus,

00:31:44.790 --> 00:31:46.620
I think it's important
to show this research

00:31:46.620 --> 00:31:49.830
because a lot of concern
about anti-Zionism takes place

00:31:49.830 --> 00:31:51.183
on college campuses.

00:31:54.210 --> 00:31:57.990
Jews, when compared to
other minority groups

00:31:57.990 --> 00:31:59.310
on college campuses,

00:31:59.310 --> 00:32:02.463
are more likely to experience hate crimes.

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:06.540
And when you look at some of the events

00:32:06.540 --> 00:32:08.100
or characteristics of a campus

00:32:08.100 --> 00:32:11.430
that might explain antisemitic
incident variation,

00:32:11.430 --> 00:32:15.120
we see that the celebration
of Israel Apartheid Week,

00:32:15.120 --> 00:32:17.742
the commemoration of
Israel Apartheid Week,

00:32:17.742 --> 00:32:20.970
and the presence of an active SJP chapter,

00:32:20.970 --> 00:32:24.570
both tied, of course, to the
Israeli-Palestinian conflict,

00:32:24.570 --> 00:32:27.723
helped to predict increased antisemitism.

00:32:29.880 --> 00:32:31.800
So, I wanna go into my own work now

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:35.580
on "Blame Attribution and the
Responsibility to Speak Out."

00:32:35.580 --> 00:32:40.350
The idea here is that when
we see Jews being targeted

00:32:40.350 --> 00:32:42.210
for the actions of Israel,

00:32:42.210 --> 00:32:46.320
is it because perpetrators blame Jews?

00:32:46.320 --> 00:32:49.890
Is there a feeling that
Jews, the diaspora,

00:32:49.890 --> 00:32:51.240
have a responsibility

00:32:51.240 --> 00:32:53.880
to speak out against
the actions of Israel?

00:32:53.880 --> 00:32:57.750
What is the mechanism
that explains the decision

00:32:57.750 --> 00:32:59.163
to engage in this behavior?

00:33:00.180 --> 00:33:03.810
So our research questions
are diaspora communities

00:33:03.810 --> 00:33:07.200
or those groups that are linked
with international actors,

00:33:07.200 --> 00:33:10.110
blamed for the actions of their home

00:33:10.110 --> 00:33:12.660
or an associated international act.

00:33:12.660 --> 00:33:15.213
Are diaspora communities or groups linked

00:33:15.213 --> 00:33:17.220
with international actors?

00:33:17.220 --> 00:33:18.810
Are they believed to have a responsibility

00:33:18.810 --> 00:33:21.000
to speak out against their homeland?

00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:22.200
And is this a case

00:33:22.200 --> 00:33:27.200
of these phenomena only
focus focusing on Jews?

00:33:27.960 --> 00:33:30.210
Our diaspora Jews uniquely blamed

00:33:30.210 --> 00:33:32.190
for the actions of their homeland

00:33:32.190 --> 00:33:35.040
and the only group responsible
to speak out against them.

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:36.713
How do we do this?

00:33:36.713 --> 00:33:38.430
We designed a survey.

00:33:38.430 --> 00:33:43.430
What we did is we utilized
these experimental vignettes.

00:33:45.030 --> 00:33:50.030
What we did is we used an
image of an apartment building

00:33:51.120 --> 00:33:54.360
and a hospital that was
damaged in the bombing,

00:33:54.360 --> 00:33:59.160
but we changed who the target
and the perpetrator was.

00:33:59.160 --> 00:34:04.160
On the first story, you
see Israel bombing Gaza.

00:34:05.790 --> 00:34:09.180
On the second, you see
Russia bombing Mariupol.

00:34:09.180 --> 00:34:14.180
On the third, you see Saudi
Arabia bombing Sana, the end.

00:34:15.180 --> 00:34:18.633
We also have another image of
ISIS operating in Afghanistan.

00:34:21.510 --> 00:34:24.157
We asked our survey respondents,

00:34:24.157 --> 00:34:27.990
"On a scale of one to 10,
how much are the diaspora

00:34:27.990 --> 00:34:30.030
or the linked group to blame

00:34:30.030 --> 00:34:34.050
for actions and policies
towards the target group?"

00:34:34.050 --> 00:34:36.000
So how might this manifest?

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:38.070
In a series of questions, right,

00:34:38.070 --> 00:34:39.150
we didn't just ask

00:34:39.150 --> 00:34:41.610
about Israel's actions
towards the Palestinians,

00:34:41.610 --> 00:34:44.940
but in those series, how much
are American Jews to blame

00:34:44.940 --> 00:34:48.390
for Israel's actions in policies
towards the Palestinians?

00:34:48.390 --> 00:34:50.940
Provide us with answer
on a scale of one to 10,

00:34:50.940 --> 00:34:54.510
same question, but designed
to also capture responsibility

00:34:54.510 --> 00:34:55.770
to speak out.

00:34:55.770 --> 00:34:56.640
What do we find?

00:34:56.640 --> 00:34:59.223
Well, when we look at
attribution of blame,

00:35:03.214 --> 00:35:08.214
only Jews, only those that were exposed

00:35:11.040 --> 00:35:13.440
to the Israel treatment,

00:35:13.440 --> 00:35:17.583
see an increase in blame
as a treatment effect.

00:35:20.370 --> 00:35:23.130
Not American Russians that were treated

00:35:23.130 --> 00:35:26.460
with the Russian treatment,

00:35:26.460 --> 00:35:29.460
not the Saudi Arabia and ISIS
treatments that we provided.

00:35:29.460 --> 00:35:31.380
This does not increase

00:35:31.380 --> 00:35:35.703
American Muslims being blamed, just Jews.

00:35:37.470 --> 00:35:39.870
This is a breakdown by ideology.

00:35:39.870 --> 00:35:42.000
I think, this is critical to show in part

00:35:42.000 --> 00:35:45.000
because one of the subfindings
that I'm happy to go

00:35:45.000 --> 00:35:45.897
into in the Q&amp;A,

00:35:45.897 --> 00:35:49.440
but what we see is that
those on the far right,

00:35:49.440 --> 00:35:53.130
actually see a reduction
in blame towards Jews,

00:35:53.130 --> 00:35:57.510
and see a significant increase
in blame towards Muslims,

00:35:57.510 --> 00:35:58.800
when they're exposed

00:35:58.800 --> 00:36:01.593
to our Israel (indistinct)
our Israel treatment.

00:36:04.920 --> 00:36:06.720
We see similar phenomena when it comes

00:36:06.720 --> 00:36:09.600
to the attribution of
responsibility to speak up.

00:36:09.600 --> 00:36:14.220
Again, only the Israel treatment results

00:36:14.220 --> 00:36:16.980
in a significant increase in the idea

00:36:16.980 --> 00:36:19.833
that Jews have a
responsibility to speak out,

00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:24.213
against that linked international
act, in this case, Israel.

00:36:25.830 --> 00:36:28.359
This is, again, a
breakdown of responsibility

00:36:28.359 --> 00:36:30.720
to speak out by ideology.

00:36:30.720 --> 00:36:31.950
I think, it's useful.

00:36:31.950 --> 00:36:34.740
On the left, we're looking
at it broken down by Jews,

00:36:34.740 --> 00:36:36.063
Muslims, and Russians.

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:41.493
On the right, we're looking
at it broken down by ideology.

00:36:46.590 --> 00:36:49.170
So, to give you a brief
finding summary here,

00:36:49.170 --> 00:36:52.140
blame of diasporas for
associated conflicts,

00:36:52.140 --> 00:36:55.110
is relatively low across the board.

00:36:55.110 --> 00:36:57.963
While applied responsibility
substantively higher,

00:36:59.296 --> 00:37:00.993
we were surprised by this.

00:37:01.950 --> 00:37:03.750
Blame and responsibility,

00:37:03.750 --> 00:37:06.993
they don't seem to operate
on the same scales.

00:37:09.120 --> 00:37:12.993
Only Jews, those exposed
to our Israel treatment,

00:37:14.220 --> 00:37:18.693
see a significant increase in
both blame and responsibility.

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:23.460
Those on the right, broadly speaking,

00:37:23.460 --> 00:37:26.310
blame Muslims more than
any other ideology,

00:37:26.310 --> 00:37:29.223
blames any other diaspora or linked group.

00:37:30.060 --> 00:37:33.107
Those on the left believe
Jews are blameworthy

00:37:33.107 --> 00:37:36.303
and responsible to speak out
more than Muslims or Russians.

00:37:37.650 --> 00:37:40.740
And building on recent
scholarship examining antisemitism

00:37:40.740 --> 00:37:41.850
on the extremes,

00:37:41.850 --> 00:37:43.770
what we're really finding here is

00:37:43.770 --> 00:37:46.890
that the effects of the
treatment are manifesting

00:37:46.890 --> 00:37:48.840
in the greatest substantive fashion

00:37:48.840 --> 00:37:51.330
on the ideological extremes.

00:37:51.330 --> 00:37:55.383
This treatment effect is
magnified on our political pulse.

00:37:56.670 --> 00:38:01.670
And I wanna introduce you to
just one other experiment,

00:38:01.927 --> 00:38:04.710
"Hate Crime Exposure &amp;
Foreign Policy Preferences."

00:38:04.710 --> 00:38:09.330
We know that hate crime
reporting has gone up.

00:38:09.330 --> 00:38:11.883
There's greater media
exposure to hate crimes.

00:38:12.720 --> 00:38:15.810
This is an image of a CNN report,

00:38:15.810 --> 00:38:19.413
examining antisemitic hate
crimes in the United States.

00:38:20.820 --> 00:38:25.820
We know how hate crimes
affect the target population,

00:38:27.480 --> 00:38:29.160
but what does it do?

00:38:29.160 --> 00:38:33.123
How does it alter policy
preferences at a national level?

00:38:37.230 --> 00:38:40.740
We ask, "How does exposure
to antisemitic hate crimes,

00:38:40.740 --> 00:38:45.570
alter foreign policy
preferences, not amongst Jews,

00:38:45.570 --> 00:38:48.930
but looking at a nationally
representative sample?"

00:38:48.930 --> 00:38:52.500
Specifically, does exposure
to antisemitic hate crime,

00:38:52.500 --> 00:38:55.233
alter foreign policy
preferences towards Israel?

00:38:56.430 --> 00:38:59.310
And even more specifically,

00:38:59.310 --> 00:39:02.310
does it manifest in certain
elements of foreign policy?

00:39:02.310 --> 00:39:04.560
Is this something that's gonna be specific

00:39:04.560 --> 00:39:06.570
to a general alliance?

00:39:06.570 --> 00:39:09.220
Something that we associate
perhaps with military aid

00:39:10.350 --> 00:39:11.763
or preferential trade?

00:39:13.530 --> 00:39:18.270
We provided a series of
vignettes to our respondents,

00:39:18.270 --> 00:39:21.600
one that focuses on a vandalism attack,

00:39:21.600 --> 00:39:23.913
one that focuses on a physical attack.

00:39:25.080 --> 00:39:29.223
We also vary what the motivation was.

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:35.480
In some cases, the perpetrator
is graffitiing death to Jews.

00:39:36.630 --> 00:39:38.760
In some cases, it's death to Israel.

00:39:38.760 --> 00:39:42.300
Same with the statement,
and the physical attack,

00:39:42.300 --> 00:39:45.123
death to Jews or death to Israel.

00:39:46.230 --> 00:39:49.740
We also change the identity
of the perpetrator.

00:39:49.740 --> 00:39:51.870
All factors that we think might help

00:39:51.870 --> 00:39:55.623
to explain variation in
foreign policy preferences.

00:39:57.870 --> 00:39:59.160
So, how do we ask this?

00:39:59.160 --> 00:40:01.740
How do we begin to assess foreign policy?

00:40:01.740 --> 00:40:02.850
We ask, in your opinion,

00:40:02.850 --> 00:40:04.620
how important is it for the United States

00:40:04.620 --> 00:40:08.040
to maintain a strong alliance with Israel?

00:40:08.040 --> 00:40:10.320
But we also ask that same question

00:40:10.320 --> 00:40:11.973
of a battery of other countries.

00:40:13.050 --> 00:40:15.450
We ask them to indicate
their level of support

00:40:15.450 --> 00:40:17.790
for US military assistance.

00:40:17.790 --> 00:40:19.830
We ask them to indicate
their level of support

00:40:19.830 --> 00:40:22.393
for preferential trade with this Israel,

00:40:22.393 --> 00:40:24.603
and again, a series of other countries.

00:40:25.920 --> 00:40:26.943
What do we find?

00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:32.100
We find that those that are exposed

00:40:32.100 --> 00:40:35.913
to hate crime across the board,

00:40:37.170 --> 00:40:39.280
find it more important

00:40:40.200 --> 00:40:43.590
to provide military assistance to Israel.

00:40:43.590 --> 00:40:45.210
They find it more important

00:40:45.210 --> 00:40:47.970
to have a general alliance with Israel.

00:40:47.970 --> 00:40:49.920
They find it more important

00:40:49.920 --> 00:40:54.123
to provide preferential
trade agreements for Israel.

00:40:56.220 --> 00:40:57.690
And we're wondering why,

00:40:57.690 --> 00:40:59.040
why is this taking place?

00:40:59.040 --> 00:40:59.990
What are we seeing?

00:41:01.950 --> 00:41:05.763
Is this manifesting because
of a sense of personal threat?

00:41:08.310 --> 00:41:10.680
So we're exposing people
to this treatment,

00:41:10.680 --> 00:41:13.980
we're exposing them to
a story of a hate crime.

00:41:13.980 --> 00:41:17.103
Does it make them feel
personally threatened to do that?

00:41:18.120 --> 00:41:20.070
We don't find considerable evidence,

00:41:20.070 --> 00:41:21.870
that's actually the case.

00:41:21.870 --> 00:41:24.090
Well, does this also manifest perhaps

00:41:24.090 --> 00:41:29.090
in feeling of increased threat
towards racial communities?

00:41:29.190 --> 00:41:31.800
This is inducing that phenomenon.

00:41:31.800 --> 00:41:35.070
Don't find considerable
evidence of that either.

00:41:35.070 --> 00:41:39.310
But what we see is that
exposing our respondents

00:41:41.460 --> 00:41:44.040
to antisemitic hate crime,

00:41:44.040 --> 00:41:47.520
does increase a general sense of threat,

00:41:47.520 --> 00:41:50.400
towards all religious communities,

00:41:50.400 --> 00:41:53.870
with the exception of the
dominant religious community

00:41:53.870 --> 00:41:55.923
in this country across the community.

00:41:57.570 --> 00:41:59.670
So those that are exposed

00:41:59.670 --> 00:42:02.430
to the hate crimes when
compared to our control group,

00:42:02.430 --> 00:42:06.810
right, feel that there's
considerable threat,

00:42:06.810 --> 00:42:09.423
towards religious communities
in the United States.

00:42:10.650 --> 00:42:14.820
So, to just go over briefly
finding summary here,

00:42:14.820 --> 00:42:19.710
exposure to antisemitic
attacks caused strong support

00:42:19.710 --> 00:42:23.640
for pro-Israel policies
relative to the control group.

00:42:23.640 --> 00:42:27.623
Outgroup exposure to hate crime
increases perceived support

00:42:27.623 --> 00:42:29.550
through the victim group,

00:42:29.550 --> 00:42:30.840
at least via preferences

00:42:30.840 --> 00:42:34.110
that we measured vis-a-vis
associated foreign policies,

00:42:34.110 --> 00:42:35.820
in their wake.

00:42:35.820 --> 00:42:37.100
And what's so critical here,

00:42:37.100 --> 00:42:39.393
is a baked normative consequence.

00:42:40.530 --> 00:42:44.673
It suggests that those that
are committing acts of hate,

00:42:46.110 --> 00:42:49.380
are actually generating policy preferences

00:42:49.380 --> 00:42:51.840
and potential support for policies

00:42:51.840 --> 00:42:53.373
that presumably they oppose.

00:42:56.650 --> 00:43:00.210
Doing a lot of really interesting
future survey work, again,

00:43:00.210 --> 00:43:03.780
trying to utilize this as an approach

00:43:03.780 --> 00:43:05.127
to examine antisemitism

00:43:05.127 --> 00:43:09.570
and the mechanisms of
antisemitism in critical ways,

00:43:09.570 --> 00:43:13.593
focusing on elements of
conspiracy, whiteness,

00:43:14.670 --> 00:43:18.060
hope and cooperation,
comparative prejudice.

00:43:18.060 --> 00:43:21.210
So not just focusing on
antisemitism specifically,

00:43:21.210 --> 00:43:23.978
utilizing specific message testing,

00:43:23.978 --> 00:43:27.453
and also focusing on the
effectiveness of interventions.

00:43:29.160 --> 00:43:30.690
And I wanna close with this work

00:43:30.690 --> 00:43:35.690
that was recently done
in our 2022 ADL survey.

00:43:37.830 --> 00:43:39.723
We asked respondents,

00:43:41.077 --> 00:43:44.770
"Do you think this
individual pictured here

00:43:46.020 --> 00:43:49.860
who may be the Jewish singer from Israel,

00:43:49.860 --> 00:43:52.020
the singer that performs
in the United States,

00:43:52.020 --> 00:43:53.340
or an Arab singer from Iraq."

00:43:53.340 --> 00:43:54.930
So again, to clarify here,

00:43:54.930 --> 00:43:57.213
some people were given this treatment,

00:43:58.440 --> 00:44:01.050
another series of people
were given this treatment,

00:44:01.050 --> 00:44:02.700
and the last series were
given this treatment,

00:44:02.700 --> 00:44:05.103
do you consider this person to be white?

00:44:10.996 --> 00:44:14.340
What we found was that those who were told

00:44:14.340 --> 00:44:18.210
that the individual picture was
a Jewish singer from Israel,

00:44:18.210 --> 00:44:20.670
were significantly more likely

00:44:20.670 --> 00:44:22.623
to identify the individual's wife.

00:44:24.240 --> 00:44:28.020
Those that were given
the singer from the USA

00:44:28.020 --> 00:44:29.793
or an Arab singer from Iraq,

00:44:32.220 --> 00:44:35.100
no significant difference between the two.

00:44:35.100 --> 00:44:38.370
White respondents who viewed
the Jewish-Israeli singer

00:44:38.370 --> 00:44:43.370
as white endorsed fewer
antisemitic tropes,

00:44:43.770 --> 00:44:46.320
than white respondents who
viewed him as non-white.

00:44:47.460 --> 00:44:50.520
And among non-white respondents,
the perceived whiteness,

00:44:50.520 --> 00:44:54.150
really didn't have a measurable
impact on antisemitism.

00:44:54.150 --> 00:44:57.120
This individual is
individual is (indistinct)

00:44:57.120 --> 00:45:01.203
He is an Iraqi-Jewish
singer living in Israel.

00:45:03.963 --> 00:45:05.220
So I'm gonna close there,

00:45:05.220 --> 00:45:08.370
just always close with images
of my cats and my daughter.

00:45:08.370 --> 00:45:10.980
Any questions or concerns, comments?

00:45:10.980 --> 00:45:12.960
Again, a lot of this
is in the early stages,

00:45:12.960 --> 00:45:15.210
so we really welcome your support

00:45:15.210 --> 00:45:16.980
and your work and your feedback.

00:45:16.980 --> 00:45:20.160
Please feel free to email
me at my Gratz email.

00:45:20.160 --> 00:45:23.013
Thank you very much for your
time, really appreciate it.

00:45:25.410 --> 00:45:27.180
- [Maurice] Thank you, Ayal,

00:45:27.180 --> 00:45:30.510
for that very interesting presentation.

00:45:30.510 --> 00:45:34.650
And I know some people have written

00:45:34.650 --> 00:45:36.570
in that there's an echo problem.

00:45:36.570 --> 00:45:38.760
Hopefully, that's been taken care of.

00:45:38.760 --> 00:45:40.200
We apologize, we're still trying

00:45:40.200 --> 00:45:41.850
to figure out this technology.

00:45:41.850 --> 00:45:43.200
- Yeah, and apologize, everybody,

00:45:43.200 --> 00:45:45.990
for my clicker happiness throughout.

00:45:45.990 --> 00:45:46.980
I'm sorry.

00:45:46.980 --> 00:45:49.020
- [Maurice] Okay, so a bunch
of questions from the audience.

00:45:49.020 --> 00:45:50.460
I have some questions too.

00:45:50.460 --> 00:45:53.760
One quick question that just came up is,

00:45:53.760 --> 00:45:55.350
could you explain the hyphen?

00:45:55.350 --> 00:45:57.570
I mean, this is, you
know, in antisemitism,

00:45:57.570 --> 00:46:00.240
this is something that always comes up

00:46:00.240 --> 00:46:01.800
in antisemitism studies.

00:46:01.800 --> 00:46:04.590
So, are you a hyphenate or no,

00:46:04.590 --> 00:46:05.580
and what does that mean?

00:46:05.580 --> 00:46:08.490
- So from the very beginning,

00:46:08.490 --> 00:46:13.293
I would write to editors that I'm okay

00:46:13.293 --> 00:46:17.970
with the decision of the journal

00:46:17.970 --> 00:46:22.953
to choose how antisemitism
should be spelled.

00:46:24.690 --> 00:46:27.990
I was never somebody that focused on this

00:46:27.990 --> 00:46:30.510
because I felt that it took away

00:46:30.510 --> 00:46:32.580
from more of the meaningful,

00:46:32.580 --> 00:46:34.530
impactful research that we could be doing,

00:46:34.530 --> 00:46:37.800
examining the manifestations
of antisemitism.

00:46:37.800 --> 00:46:39.720
I believe there is a place

00:46:39.720 --> 00:46:42.702
for definitional concerns of antisemitism,

00:46:42.702 --> 00:46:46.380
and I applaud the scholars
that engage in it,

00:46:46.380 --> 00:46:49.410
but it's not the area of focus for me.

00:46:49.410 --> 00:46:54.390
The origin of this is commonly expressed

00:46:54.390 --> 00:46:59.370
as related to this notion of
there being no Semitic people.

00:46:59.370 --> 00:47:04.110
That being Semitic is actually
a language orientation.

00:47:04.110 --> 00:47:06.803
And therefore by getting rid of the hyphen

00:47:06.803 --> 00:47:08.940
and making it antisemitism, right,

00:47:08.940 --> 00:47:11.910
we're making this a phenomenon
that's specific to choose.

00:47:11.910 --> 00:47:15.630
But I'll go back to the
comment from the slide

00:47:15.630 --> 00:47:17.880
where I talked about this very subject,

00:47:17.880 --> 00:47:20.430
which is that many people
in the United States,

00:47:20.430 --> 00:47:23.520
a huge proportion of people in
the United States don't know

00:47:23.520 --> 00:47:26.010
what antisemitism is.

00:47:26.010 --> 00:47:28.950
So this is part of a broader debate

00:47:28.950 --> 00:47:30.000
that we need to be having

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:33.183
in terms of clarifying the
visibility of the subject.

00:47:34.350 --> 00:47:35.670
- [Maurice] Okay, great.

00:47:35.670 --> 00:47:39.060
So, a question about AI.

00:47:39.060 --> 00:47:39.990
- Yes.
- [Maurice] Yeah.

00:47:39.990 --> 00:47:41.220
So, can you talk a little bit

00:47:41.220 --> 00:47:43.830
about AI-generated antisemitism?

00:47:43.830 --> 00:47:46.260
Is this posing an even greater risk than

00:47:46.260 --> 00:47:49.620
what we were experiencing before?

00:47:49.620 --> 00:47:51.990
And maybe just tell for audience members

00:47:51.990 --> 00:47:56.990
who aren't familiar with AI,
what we're talking about here.

00:47:57.060 --> 00:48:00.780
- So first of all, I am
no expert in AI myself,

00:48:00.780 --> 00:48:02.250
so let me make that clear.

00:48:02.250 --> 00:48:05.620
There are some individuals that
are working on this research

00:48:06.720 --> 00:48:08.310
that know it much better than I do.

00:48:08.310 --> 00:48:10.440
But the idea here is that, in fact,

00:48:10.440 --> 00:48:14.820
you can take the correlates
of antisemitic propaganda

00:48:14.820 --> 00:48:17.433
that work the best in terms of influence,

00:48:18.933 --> 00:48:20.673
in terms of recruitment,

00:48:22.080 --> 00:48:27.080
and generate truly excellent
antisemitic propaganda.

00:48:29.250 --> 00:48:31.530
It can help you,
generative AI can help you

00:48:31.530 --> 00:48:34.020
in terms of finding the best avenue

00:48:34.020 --> 00:48:36.750
in which to advertise this
or present this material.

00:48:36.750 --> 00:48:41.400
It can help you in recruiting
specific individuals.

00:48:41.400 --> 00:48:44.160
By testing this back and forth,

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:45.990
you can provide generative AI,

00:48:45.990 --> 00:48:50.760
a variety of different
elements of propaganda,

00:48:50.760 --> 00:48:52.233
and it can refine it for you.

00:48:53.250 --> 00:48:54.690
I can tell you that this is something

00:48:54.690 --> 00:48:58.255
that's of deep concern in
the intelligence community,

00:48:58.255 --> 00:49:03.255
and it's worthy of studying
and evaluating further.

00:49:03.300 --> 00:49:06.060
I can tell you that
definitely there are actors

00:49:06.060 --> 00:49:09.270
that are utilizing AI for the purpose

00:49:09.270 --> 00:49:12.780
of making antisemitic propaganda

00:49:12.780 --> 00:49:16.803
and rhetoric more impactful.

00:49:18.134 --> 00:49:19.440
- [Maurice] Thank you.

00:49:19.440 --> 00:49:24.440
So a few questions actually
about your surveys,

00:49:24.750 --> 00:49:29.750
and are there generational
differences in how people respond

00:49:31.500 --> 00:49:32.400
to the survey?

00:49:32.400 --> 00:49:34.080
How do you control for that?

00:49:34.080 --> 00:49:37.410
- Well, we always control
for age, for education,

00:49:37.410 --> 00:49:40.260
anything related to SCS,
we control for religion,

00:49:40.260 --> 00:49:42.900
we control for any of
the demographic factors,

00:49:42.900 --> 00:49:46.050
the geographic factors that are critical

00:49:46.050 --> 00:49:49.110
in designing a nationally
representative sample.

00:49:49.110 --> 00:49:51.941
There are some interesting
generational effects.

00:49:51.941 --> 00:49:56.941
We see that there is not
always this linear effect

00:49:58.020 --> 00:49:59.340
as it relates to age.

00:49:59.340 --> 00:50:01.870
Many people assume that antisemitism,

00:50:01.870 --> 00:50:06.570
is going to be the highest
among the oldest population.

00:50:06.570 --> 00:50:08.460
That is not always the case.

00:50:08.460 --> 00:50:10.440
Depends on the specific element

00:50:10.440 --> 00:50:13.110
of antisemitism you're trying to measure.

00:50:13.110 --> 00:50:15.690
For example, when it comes
to anti-Zionism, right,

00:50:15.690 --> 00:50:18.180
anti-Zionism, and again,

00:50:18.180 --> 00:50:20.100
not that all anti-Zionism is antisemitism,

00:50:20.100 --> 00:50:23.480
but anti-Zionist, antisemitism, right,

00:50:23.480 --> 00:50:28.320
is actually lowest amongst
the oldest generations,

00:50:28.320 --> 00:50:29.640
and it's highest amongst the younger.

00:50:29.640 --> 00:50:32.190
I think that comes as no
surprise to many people,

00:50:32.190 --> 00:50:36.090
but elements of antisemitism
that are related to power,

00:50:36.090 --> 00:50:37.830
related to economics, right?

00:50:37.830 --> 00:50:42.060
That's oftentimes a
millennial phenomenon, right?

00:50:42.060 --> 00:50:45.480
In part because of the exogenous factors

00:50:45.480 --> 00:50:48.453
that they experienced as it
relates to the procession.

00:50:49.860 --> 00:50:50.693
- [Maurice] Okay.

00:50:50.693 --> 00:50:55.487
And so, would you say that
there are different antisemitism

00:50:56.580 --> 00:50:59.220
in like, we're talking about
the American context right now?

00:50:59.220 --> 00:51:00.300
- Sure.
- [Maurice] So, for instance,

00:51:00.300 --> 00:51:04.590
we have what's going on
arguably on college campuses,

00:51:04.590 --> 00:51:07.890
but that seems to me anyway very different

00:51:07.890 --> 00:51:11.100
from Orthodox Jews on the streets

00:51:11.100 --> 00:51:13.380
in Brooklyn getting attacked.

00:51:13.380 --> 00:51:17.250
So, how do your surveys get

00:51:17.250 --> 00:51:19.710
at those different kinds of antisemitism?

00:51:19.710 --> 00:51:21.995
Because it strikes me that, you know,

00:51:21.995 --> 00:51:24.090
it's picking up something,

00:51:24.090 --> 00:51:27.480
but it's maybe not picking
up those differences.

00:51:27.480 --> 00:51:29.820
- So yeah, I think that
there are different elements

00:51:29.820 --> 00:51:33.060
of why Jews might be targeted
for specific behavior.

00:51:33.060 --> 00:51:34.530
First of all, when we think

00:51:34.530 --> 00:51:36.480
about the broader structure possible,

00:51:36.480 --> 00:51:39.540
you have to be able to
identify a perpetrator, right?

00:51:39.540 --> 00:51:43.950
If you're able to identify a target,

00:51:43.950 --> 00:51:46.020
perpetrator needs to identify a target.

00:51:46.020 --> 00:51:48.030
When you identify a
specific target, right,

00:51:48.030 --> 00:51:49.920
when we think about Jews, right,

00:51:49.920 --> 00:51:54.330
not all Jews are going to be
identifiable to a perpetrator.

00:51:54.330 --> 00:51:56.010
There's not an even distribution

00:51:56.010 --> 00:51:58.020
of Jewish institutions
that can be targeted.

00:51:58.020 --> 00:51:59.670
So specific areas

00:51:59.670 --> 00:52:01.530
where there's a higher
concentration views,

00:52:01.530 --> 00:52:04.020
including American college campuses,

00:52:04.020 --> 00:52:05.730
are gonna see, let's say,

00:52:05.730 --> 00:52:08.790
an increased likelihood
of antisemitic hate crime

00:52:08.790 --> 00:52:10.020
as a consequence.

00:52:10.020 --> 00:52:12.240
That's going to be different, right,

00:52:12.240 --> 00:52:14.433
than areas that have fewer Jews.

00:52:15.270 --> 00:52:17.500
When it comes to thinking about this topic

00:52:19.830 --> 00:52:21.570
from a visibly Jewish standpoint,

00:52:21.570 --> 00:52:26.553
thinking about let's say
orthodox or rhetoric communities,

00:52:27.660 --> 00:52:32.660
yes, we note that there's
higher likelihood of hate crimes

00:52:32.790 --> 00:52:34.620
that are targeting those individuals.

00:52:34.620 --> 00:52:38.160
When asking those individuals
about their experience,

00:52:38.160 --> 00:52:39.600
they're more likely to say

00:52:39.600 --> 00:52:44.070
that they experience antisemitic
rhetoric being the victims

00:52:44.070 --> 00:52:45.960
of an antisemitic incident.

00:52:45.960 --> 00:52:47.910
So again, this all plays a role

00:52:47.910 --> 00:52:50.727
in the likelihood of a primary state.

00:52:50.727 --> 00:52:51.750
- [Maurice] And do you see

00:52:51.750 --> 00:52:55.500
that there's more measurable antisemitism

00:52:55.500 --> 00:52:59.190
on college campuses with
greater Jewish populations?

00:52:59.190 --> 00:53:03.000
- So we find that yes, the
larger the Jewish population,

00:53:03.000 --> 00:53:07.200
the greater the number of
incidents that are being reported.

00:53:07.200 --> 00:53:09.240
We find that the proportion of Jews,

00:53:09.240 --> 00:53:11.640
so when we think about it
from a percentage standpoint,

00:53:11.640 --> 00:53:13.230
that's not a great predictor

00:53:13.230 --> 00:53:15.750
of explaining antisemitic
incidents on campus.

00:53:15.750 --> 00:53:18.450
So you need a certain critical mass Jews

00:53:18.450 --> 00:53:22.560
to experience considerable antisemitism.

00:53:22.560 --> 00:53:25.920
- [Maurice] But you do find
that on college campuses,

00:53:25.920 --> 00:53:28.470
and that's true kind of across the board.

00:53:28.470 --> 00:53:33.470
Like, it really correlates that, or is it,

00:53:33.750 --> 00:53:36.180
how have you guys, you know,

00:53:36.180 --> 00:53:38.553
who's studying that and what have they?

00:53:40.200 --> 00:53:41.250
- There's lots of research

00:53:41.250 --> 00:53:43.560
that's really trying
to examine this issue.

00:53:43.560 --> 00:53:47.460
The work that I did was a project for AN.

00:53:47.460 --> 00:53:52.080
What I did is I looked at
Hillel International's report

00:53:52.080 --> 00:53:55.320
on Jewish populations across
different institutions.

00:53:55.320 --> 00:53:57.750
We looked at the incident rates

00:53:57.750 --> 00:53:59.193
that have been reported,

00:54:00.210 --> 00:54:03.900
and we evaluated whether or
not there was a relationship,

00:54:03.900 --> 00:54:06.590
between the size of the Jewish population

00:54:06.590 --> 00:54:10.230
at that institution as well as prestige,

00:54:10.230 --> 00:54:14.700
as well as geographic
characteristics, acceptance rates.

00:54:14.700 --> 00:54:17.547
We looked at a variety
of different cohorts,

00:54:17.547 --> 00:54:18.630
but we found that one

00:54:18.630 --> 00:54:20.640
of the most important predictive factors

00:54:20.640 --> 00:54:22.950
of whether or not an institution,

00:54:22.950 --> 00:54:26.040
would see heightened
antisemitism was, of course,

00:54:26.040 --> 00:54:28.050
the size of the Jewish population.

00:54:28.050 --> 00:54:29.490
- [Maurice] Okay, here's a question

00:54:29.490 --> 00:54:32.100
that I'm not sure you'll
be able to answer,

00:54:32.100 --> 00:54:34.530
but I'd love to just hear
what you think, which is,

00:54:34.530 --> 00:54:36.600
if Israel weren't in the picture,

00:54:36.600 --> 00:54:39.720
would the incidents of
antisemitism still be on the rise?

00:54:39.720 --> 00:54:42.240
Is it impossible to separate the two?

00:54:42.240 --> 00:54:44.673
- I love and hate counterfactuals.

00:54:46.500 --> 00:54:49.500
If Israel wasn't here, would
incidents still be on the rise?

00:54:49.500 --> 00:54:51.420
What I'd say is that it's really important

00:54:51.420 --> 00:54:54.150
that we don't think about anti-Zionism

00:54:54.150 --> 00:54:55.590
and anti-Israel sentiment.

00:54:55.590 --> 00:54:57.300
It's something that exists exclusively

00:54:57.300 --> 00:54:59.370
with one ideological domain.

00:54:59.370 --> 00:55:01.250
One of the things that I often do,

00:55:01.250 --> 00:55:02.390
I didn't do it in this presentation,

00:55:02.390 --> 00:55:07.390
is I show two vehemently
anti-Zionist quotes.

00:55:07.650 --> 00:55:09.780
One coming from Louis Farrakhan

00:55:09.780 --> 00:55:11.580
and the other coming
from Matthew Heimbach,

00:55:11.580 --> 00:55:14.280
who's a relatively infamous neo-Nazi here

00:55:14.280 --> 00:55:15.630
in the United States.

00:55:15.630 --> 00:55:17.160
Again, the idea here is

00:55:17.160 --> 00:55:20.810
that antisemitism
doesn't exist exclusively

00:55:28.470 --> 00:55:31.773
in specific ideological
domains, it doesn't.

00:55:33.180 --> 00:55:35.340
I think, anti-Zionism specifically,

00:55:35.340 --> 00:55:39.220
and anti-Zionism antisemitism
is a convenient way

00:55:42.806 --> 00:55:45.270
to manifest certain
elements of antisemitism

00:55:45.270 --> 00:55:47.133
in a more socially acceptable way.

00:55:48.030 --> 00:55:49.860
It's not always that case,

00:55:49.860 --> 00:55:52.383
but I think it's a useful
tool for that reason.

00:55:53.725 --> 00:55:57.540
If Israel wasn't here,
would we see it on the rise?

00:55:57.540 --> 00:55:59.460
I don't know, obviously,

00:55:59.460 --> 00:56:02.430
and I would refute anybody
that says that they do.

00:56:02.430 --> 00:56:05.043
But antisemitism has
existed for millennia,

00:56:06.330 --> 00:56:08.820
so I imagine it would still be here.

00:56:08.820 --> 00:56:12.170
And the most infamous
incidents of antisemitism

00:56:12.170 --> 00:56:14.853
in this country existed
part of creation of Israel.

00:56:15.690 --> 00:56:18.420
- [Maurice] And so a lot of your studies,

00:56:18.420 --> 00:56:21.750
are the ones you showed today reflected,

00:56:21.750 --> 00:56:22.980
we're trying to control

00:56:22.980 --> 00:56:26.550
or trying to examine incident
antisemitic feelings,

00:56:26.550 --> 00:56:30.750
according to political positions.

00:56:30.750 --> 00:56:32.070
- Yeah.
- [Maurice] But what about,

00:56:32.070 --> 00:56:35.280
are you studying or is
anybody studying the effect

00:56:35.280 --> 00:56:40.280
of family background or
levels of stress and anxiety

00:56:41.310 --> 00:56:44.040
in the home or things like that

00:56:44.040 --> 00:56:48.000
and how that contributes to antisemitic?

00:56:48.000 --> 00:56:50.850
So more kind of psychological profiles.

00:56:50.850 --> 00:56:51.780
- Of the perpetrators?

00:56:51.780 --> 00:56:53.950
- [Maurice] Of perpetrators, yeah.

00:56:53.950 --> 00:56:55.950
- Yeah, that's a really
interesting question.

00:56:55.950 --> 00:56:58.830
I think, it's hard to get
access to that data, right?

00:56:58.830 --> 00:57:00.780
First thing, when we
think about hate crimes

00:57:00.780 --> 00:57:02.580
and reported hate crimes,

00:57:02.580 --> 00:57:06.213
we look at reports that are
coming from policing agencies.

00:57:07.110 --> 00:57:08.850
The number of hate crime prosecutions

00:57:08.850 --> 00:57:11.820
in this country is remarkably low.

00:57:11.820 --> 00:57:14.640
So those that we can
really definitively say,

00:57:14.640 --> 00:57:16.320
are perpetrators of a hate crime,

00:57:16.320 --> 00:57:20.040
using that definition of being
convicted of a hate crime.

00:57:20.040 --> 00:57:23.970
Profiles on that sense are
really mostly available

00:57:23.970 --> 00:57:26.760
in a comparative sense,
in a qualitative sense,

00:57:26.760 --> 00:57:28.233
less so a quantitative sense.

00:57:29.250 --> 00:57:33.060
Are there specific elements
of someone's upbringing

00:57:33.060 --> 00:57:35.820
that makes them more likely
to potentially engage

00:57:35.820 --> 00:57:37.623
in antisemitic behavior?

00:57:38.850 --> 00:57:41.280
We can look to some of the work

00:57:41.280 --> 00:57:44.223
from perpetrators themselves
reporting on this issue,

00:57:46.260 --> 00:57:49.930
sense of loneliness, and needing to belong

00:57:51.780 --> 00:57:54.510
in a non-nuclear family structure.

00:57:54.510 --> 00:57:55.343
These are things

00:57:55.343 --> 00:57:59.730
that explain hate crime
perpetration potentially,

00:57:59.730 --> 00:58:02.220
but they also explain
all crime perpetration.

00:58:02.220 --> 00:58:05.070
So it's really hard to disentangle this.

00:58:05.070 --> 00:58:07.920
- [Maurice] Yeah, and
I've heard Günther Jikeli,

00:58:07.920 --> 00:58:11.340
you know, some of his research
which has shown that maybe,

00:58:11.340 --> 00:58:15.150
you know, counterintuitively, you know,

00:58:15.150 --> 00:58:19.530
levels of poverty didn't really
see in studying antisemitism

00:58:19.530 --> 00:58:23.520
in European-Muslim communities.

00:58:23.520 --> 00:58:25.560
It wasn't correlated necessarily

00:58:25.560 --> 00:58:27.510
with socioeconomic background,

00:58:27.510 --> 00:58:32.287
but much more with exposure to
extreme religious ideologies.

00:58:32.287 --> 00:58:35.460
So, okay, we're basically out of time,

00:58:35.460 --> 00:58:39.330
but I wanted to end on
maybe a more hopeful note

00:58:39.330 --> 00:58:44.330
by what research is being done
and which solutions work best

00:58:45.000 --> 00:58:50.000
for solving antisemitism or making it

00:58:51.120 --> 00:58:52.590
at least a little bit better?

00:58:52.590 --> 00:58:54.600
- I think, there's a really big push now

00:58:54.600 --> 00:58:58.560
to try to figure out
antisemitism interventions,

00:58:58.560 --> 00:59:03.150
ways in which we can reduce
both the causes of antisemitism,

00:59:03.150 --> 00:59:04.650
how we can generate policy

00:59:04.650 --> 00:59:07.170
that prevents the
manifestations of antisemitism,

00:59:07.170 --> 00:59:09.570
the behavior, and then also, right,

00:59:09.570 --> 00:59:14.570
how do we assess the
effectiveness of these elements,

00:59:14.760 --> 00:59:18.000
of these policies, of these interventions?

00:59:18.000 --> 00:59:20.670
A lot of this is being
done through education.

00:59:20.670 --> 00:59:23.190
There's actually really
amazing work that's being done

00:59:23.190 --> 00:59:25.440
by Joshua Cowell and David Brooklyn,

00:59:25.440 --> 00:59:27.660
that I'll let them speak to

00:59:27.660 --> 00:59:29.340
because I don't believe it's yet out.

00:59:29.340 --> 00:59:33.090
But they're doing fantastic
work evaluating the various ways

00:59:33.090 --> 00:59:36.516
in which one can intervene
to reduce antisemitism

00:59:36.516 --> 00:59:39.990
from both an educational
and a policy perspective.

00:59:39.990 --> 00:59:42.510
There's a question of
whether contact is going

00:59:42.510 --> 00:59:45.153
to be effective, if it's a
specific-type of contact.

00:59:46.440 --> 00:59:48.712
Again, I showed you some of the work

00:59:48.712 --> 00:59:51.799
on that from the Polish contexts.

00:59:51.799 --> 00:59:53.670
I can tell you that from working

00:59:53.670 --> 00:59:55.650
with some scholars in Norway,

00:59:55.650 --> 00:59:57.540
the Pathfinders programs,

00:59:57.540 --> 01:00:01.050
specifically what they've
done is expose individuals

01:00:01.050 --> 01:00:02.730
to Norwegian Jews,

01:00:02.730 --> 01:00:06.420
communities that normally don't
have Norwegian Jews focusing

01:00:06.420 --> 01:00:09.360
on the Norwegian elements

01:00:09.360 --> 01:00:11.940
of that Jewish individual's experience.

01:00:11.940 --> 01:00:15.300
And they see a considerable
reduction in antisemitism,

01:00:15.300 --> 01:00:18.390
but also, prejudice towards
other minority groups

01:00:18.390 --> 01:00:20.280
and marginalized groups in Norway.

01:00:20.280 --> 01:00:23.010
So there are avenues
that are being explored,

01:00:23.010 --> 01:00:24.690
but it's widely understood,

01:00:24.690 --> 01:00:27.150
amongst the scholars setting antisemitism

01:00:27.150 --> 01:00:28.768
that this is the area

01:00:28.768 --> 01:00:31.290
that's received the
least rigorous attention.

01:00:31.290 --> 01:00:32.123
- [Maurice] Okay, terrific.

01:00:32.123 --> 01:00:35.790
Well, thank you so much for
your really enlightening talk,

01:00:35.790 --> 01:00:38.820
and thank you to everyone in the audience

01:00:38.820 --> 01:00:40.140
for your questions.

01:00:40.140 --> 01:00:41.220
I urge everybody,

01:00:41.220 --> 01:00:43.200
if you're not ready to please get

01:00:43.200 --> 01:00:46.500
on the GIPSA or YPSA mailing list.

01:00:46.500 --> 01:00:51.500
We have a lot of interesting
programming this year,

01:00:51.540 --> 01:00:55.590
and look forward to seeing
you all future events.

01:00:55.590 --> 01:00:58.833
So please join me in
thanking Ayal Feinberg.

